\description{
Draws a site-by-species response matrix from a latent factor model
and returns it in the long format \code{\link[=jsdgam]{jsdgam()}} takes. Each species
gets its own intercept and its own slope on a site-level
covariate. The species share \code{n_lv} latent factors, whose loadings
set the residual correlation between them.

This is the non-detection counterpart to \code{\link[=sim_closure_unit_data]{sim_closure_unit_data()}},
which simulates the same latent factor structure for the
occupancy and N-mixture families.
}
\details{
The linear predictor for species \code{k} at site \code{i} is
\code{intercept[k] + env_slope[k] * env[i] + Z[k, ] \%*\% lv[i, ]}, where
\code{Z} holds the loadings and \code{lv} the site scores. The residual
covariance the model targets is \verb{Z Z'}, plus a diagonal of squared
per-species scales for \code{\link[=mvn]{mvn()}} and \code{\link[=mvt]{mvt()}}.

How that predictor becomes a response depends on the family.
Families whose species are drawn independently, such as
\code{\link[brms:brmsfamily]{brms::negbinomial()}} or \code{\link[stats:family]{stats::poisson()}}, take one draw per
species. \code{\link[=mvn]{mvn()}} and \code{\link[=mvt]{mvt()}} add a per-species residual. The
composition families draw a site's species together. \code{\link[=diri]{diri()}}
normalises gamma variates. \code{\link[=multi]{multi()}} draws counts against a fixed
site total. \code{\link[=categ]{categ()}} draws one species per site as a one-hot row.

A composition family identifies its loadings only up to a shift
shared by the species, which is the subspace mvgam's
\code{sum_to_zero_vector} parameterisation covers. The loadings are
centred for those families, which puts the simulated truth in the
same subspace a fit explores.

How much of the factor structure survives into the response
depends on the observation noise. The loadings are drawn with
standard deviation 0.7. \code{\link[=mvn]{mvn()}} and \code{\link[=mvt]{mvt()}} take their per-species
scale from \code{Psi}, which defaults to 0.5. Every other family takes
the scale its own simulator defaults to. A gaussian's \code{sigma}
defaults to 1, which can hide the species correlations from the
data. Pass \code{family_pars = list(sigma = 0.5)} for a gaussian whose
correlations a fit can recover.
